<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>When the two SCPs are brought together, they become incredibly powerful, virtually unstoppable, until they are separated once again.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>I feel like this is the least interesting reason to keep them apart&#8230; remember quite a few anomalies aren’t deadly or dangerous at all, they’re contained because they threaten the veil. I think a different reason might be more interesting, either they don’t know what will happen or the anomalies will actually negate each other and then lead to a small reality reshuffling.. one or two of these might not be a big deal, but all the anomalies? That would be chaos</em></p>
<ul>
<li>another problem I have is the timing: many anomalies are things that have existed for hundreds or thousands of years before the Foundation found them&#8230; why didn’t their “soulmate” anomaly merely conjoin with them during all that time</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>what about the hundreds of anomalies that the foundation doesn’t really contain at all? Either they can’t or the only thing they’re doing is making sure people don’t know about it, but they don’t physically contain it&#8230;</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>how do you intend to show the anomalies wanting to come together? Each anomaly is different, so you can’t just have a one size fits all event that brings them together</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>I feel like maybe the right answer isn’t each mainlist anomaly has another pair, I feel like this is so symmetrical as to beggar belief&#8230; might want to consider a different reason for why this “other” foundation exists</li>
</ul>
</div>
