			<div class="content" id="post-content-4864230">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The anomalies are not naturally drawn to each other. They can exist in entirely opposing sections of the world and may continue along with their anomalous existence completely unaware of the existence of the other and feel no urge to be brought together.<br />
Anomalies that don't possess physical bodies and a semblance of sapience don't have counterparts. To clarify, an anomaly must be a living being in order to have a counterpart, and thus places or events do not possess one where people and animals do.</p>
<p>What brings them together is an anomalous annual event wherein the two foundations &quot;meet&quot; each other and come increasingly closer to merging at the will of SCP staff, who become more and more difficult to stop or dissuade with each occurrence. If they weren't separated in two, it would be much easier for the staff to end up bringing these two anomalies together; A mismanagement during transfers would be fairly detrimental.</p>
<p>What I'm trying to get at is that when some of the more powerful anomalies come together, especially ones with a distaste for life, it would invariably lead to a K-Class scenario. I intend to focus on SK, where the anomalies either take over the planet or destroy humanity, but of course there are others with other intents and some without intents that would ruin reality regardless. What I want to try to do is allow the reader some room to fill in the blanks and come up with their own counterparts or scenarios. What would 507's hightened anomalous powers be if he came into contact with his entirely-immobile counterpart? Instead of him shifting into other realities, does he bring those realities to us?</p>
<p>I guess I just feel as though if I answered every little thing about what could happen, it might kill some of the magic for the reader. I'm trying to figure out how to leave things just open-ended enough to leave the reader with a solid understanding of how things work but also with a dread of the possible counterparts, and powers granted to the possible couples.</p>
<p>Edit: I should mention that I am most certainly going to mention the potential for complete restructuring, shuffling, or destruction of reality, but I don't intend to make it a major focus, just a lingering potential threat.</p>
</div>
